 Kyoto, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Laboratory for Cancer Biology, Department of Biotechnology and Genetic Engineering, Jahangirnagar University, Savar, Dhaka, Bangladesh."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5440-0315"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"11","journalIssueId":3187190,"dateOfPublication":"2021 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2021,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bio Protoc","title":"Bio-protocol","essn":"2331-8325","issn":"2331-8325","isoabbreviation":"Bio Protoc","nlmid":"101635102"},"printPublicationDate":"2021-04-01"},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"e3982","abstractText":"Muscimol is a psychoactive isoxazole derived from the mushroom <i>Amanita muscaria</i>. As a potent GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor agonist, muscimol suppresses the activity of the central nervous system, reduces anxiety and induces sleep. We investigated the effects of muscimol on <i>Drosophila</i> behavior. <i>Drosophila</i> behavioral assays are powerful tools that are used to assess neural functions by focusing on specific changes in selected behavior, with the hypothesis that this behavioral change is due to alteration of the underlying neural function of interest. In this study, we developed a comparatively simple and cost-effective method for feeding adult flies muscimol, a pharmacologically active compound, and for quantifying the phenotypes of \"resting\" and \"grooming+walking\". This protocol may provide researchers with a convenient method to characterize small molecule-induced behavioral output in flies.","affiliation":"Division of Food Science and Biotechnology, Graduate School of Agriculture, Kyoto University, Kyoto, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["methods-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Drosophila","Behavior","muscimol","Anxiety","Gabaa","Neural Function"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21769/BioProtoc.3982"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8160546"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8160546?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21769/bioprotoc.3982"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2021-06-14","dateOfRevision":"2022-04-21","electronicPublicationDate":"2021-04-20","firstPublicationDate":"2021-04-20","firstIndexDate":"2021-06-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-04","embargoDate":"2022-04-20","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"35663141","source":"MED","pmid":"35663141","pmcid":"PMC9060089","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC9060089"]},"doi":"10.1002/ski2.53","title":"The spectrum of skin-related conditions in primary care during 2015-2019-A Finnish nationwide database study.","authorString":"Salava A, Oker-Blom A, Remitz A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Salava A","lastName":"Salava","firstName":"A","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5471-5894"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology and Allergology Helsinki University Hospital Helsinki Finland."}]}},{"fullName":"Oker-Blom A","lastName":"Oker-Blom","firstName":"A","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology and Allergology Helsinki University Hospital Helsinki Finland."}]}},{"fullName":"Remitz A","lastName":"Remitz","firstName":"A","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7224-5662"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology and Allergology Helsinki University Hospital Helsinki Finland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5471-5894"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7224-5662"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"1","journalIssueId":3262623,"dateOfPublication":"2021 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2021,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Skin Health Dis","title":"Skin health and disease","essn":"2690-442X","issn":"2690-442X","isoabbreviation":"Skin Health Dis","nlmid":"9918227353706676"},"printPublicationDate":"2021-09-01"},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"e53","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Skin-related conditions are the frequent cause of doctors' consultations in primary care.<h4>Methods</h4>Based on nationwide data bank information of the Finnish Institute for Health and Welfare, we analysed the 20 most frequent main diagnoses for each ICD-10 category of all general practitioners' visits in the public health care in Finland over the years 2015-2019.<h4>Results</h4>The total amount of doctor's visits was 19 204 613 of which 1 489 228 consultations (7.80%) had a skin-related condition as the main diagnosis. The most frequent skin-related conditions were eczematous eruptions, bacterial skin infections and benign skin neoplasms accounting for 749 351 consultations (50.32%). The spectrum of skin-related conditions was diverse, with a large quantity of rarer diagnoses. Some diagnoses showed significant proportional changes.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The results demonstrate that a limited amount of conditions comprises most of the skin-related consultations in primary care in Finland. Undergraduate education in dermatology should concentrate on the most frequent conditions seen by general practitioners, but also address the wide range of skin problems.","affiliation":"Department of Dermatology and Allergology Helsinki University Hospital Helsinki Finland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"This article received no specific grant from any funding agency in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ski2.53"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9060089"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9060089?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":17,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2022-06-06","dateOfRevision":"2022-07-16","electronicPublicationDate":"2021-06-05","firstPublicationDate":"2021-06-05","firstIndexDate":"2022-06-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2022-06-06","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"37772058","source":"MED","pmid":"37772058","pmcid":"PMC10522913","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC10522913"]},"doi":"10.3389/fphys.2023.1207705","title":"Probiotic effects of <i>Lactococcus lactis</i> and <i>Leuconostoc mesenteroides</i> on stress and longevity in <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i>.","authorString":"Stover MA, Tinoco-Bravo B, Shults CA, Marouk S, Deole R, Manjarrez JR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Stover MA","lastName":"Stover","firstName":"Mylissa A","initials":"MA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biochemistry and Microbiology Department, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Tinoco-Bravo B","lastName":"Tinoco-Bravo","firstName":"Brenda","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biochemistry and Microbiology Department, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Shults CA","lastName":"Shults","firstName":"Crystal A","initials":"CA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biochemistry and Microbiology Department, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Marouk S","lastName":"Marouk","firstName":"Sydney","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biochemistry and Microbiology Department, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Deole R","lastName":"Deole","firstName":"Ratnakar","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biochemistry and Microbiology Department, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Manjarrez JR","lastName":"Manjarrez","firstName":"Jacob R","initials":"JR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8428-8659"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biochemistry and Microbiology Department, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8428-8659"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":3497052,"dateOfPublication":"2023 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2023,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Physiol","title":"Frontiers in physiology","essn":"1664-042X","issn":"1664-042X","isoabbreviation":"Front Physiol","nlmid":"101549006"},"printPublicationDate":"2023-01-01"},"pubYear":"2023","pageInfo":"1207705","abstractText":"The short lifespan of <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i> enables the efficient investigation of probiotic interventions affecting stress and longevity involving the potential therapeutic value of <i>Lactococcus lactis</i> and <i>Leuconostoc mesenteroides</i> isolated from organic basil. The lactic acid bacteria were cultured from the produce collected from a local grocery store in Tulsa, Oklahoma, and then identified through 16S rDNA sequencing and biochemical tests. To dive deep into this analysis for potential probiotic therapy, we used fluorescent reporters that allow us to assess the differential induction of multiple stress pathways such as oxidative stress and the cytoplasmic, endoplasmic reticulum, and the mitochondrial unfolded protein response. This is combined with the classic health span measurements of survival, development, and fecundity, allowing a wide range of organismal observations of the different communities of microbes supported by probiotic supplementation with <i>Lactococcus lactis</i> and <i>Leuconostoc mesenteroides</i>. These strains were initially assessed in relation to the <i>Escherichia coli</i> feeding strain OP50 and the <i>C. elegans</i> microbiome. The supplementation showed a reduction in the median lifespan of the worms colonized within the microbiome. This was unsurprising, as negative results are common when probiotics are introduced into healthy microbiomes. To further assess the supplementation potential of these strains on an unhealthy (undifferentiated) microbiome, the typical axenic <i>C. elegans</i> diet, OP50, was used to simulate this single-species biome. The addition of lactic acid bacteria to OP50 led to a significant improvement in the median and overall survival in simulated biomes, indicating their potential in probiotic therapy. The study analyzed the supplemented cultures in terms of <i>C. elegans</i>' morphology, locomotor behavior, reproduction, and stress responses, revealing unique characteristics and stress response patterns for each group. As the microbiome's influence on the health span gains interest, the study aims to understand the microbiome relationships that result in differential stress resistance and lifespans by supplementing microbiomes with <i>Lactococcus lactis</i> and <i>Leuconostoc mesenteroides</i> isolated from organic basil in <i>C. elegans</i>.","affiliation":"Biochemistry and Microbiology Department, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"P40 OD010440","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Longevity","Stress","Lactococcus lactis","Caenorhabditis elegans","Probiotic","Lifespan","Leuconostoc Mesenteroides"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2023.1207705"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10522913"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10522913?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":3,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2023-09-29","dateOfRevision":"2023-10-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2023-09-12","firstPublicationDate":"2023-09-12","firstIndexDate":"2023-09-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"31500368","source":"MED","pmid":"31500368","pmcid":"PMC6770392","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC6770392"]},"doi":"10.3390/nu11092146","title":"Worm-Based Alternate Assessment of Probiotic Intervention against Gut Barrier Infection.","authorString":"Kim J, Moon Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kim J","lastName":"Kim","firstName":"Juil","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan 50612, Korea. 1022myths@hanmail.net."}]}},{"fullName":"Moon Y","lastName":"Moon","firstName":"Yuseok","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8686-8166"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan 50612, Korea. moon@pnu.edu."},{"affiliation":"College of Information and Biomedical Engineering, Pusan National University, Yangsan 50612, Korea. moon@pnu.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8686-8166"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["related_data","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"11","journalIssueId":2854455,"dateOfPublication":"2019 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2019,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nutrients","title":"Nutrients","essn":"2072-6643","issn":"2072-6643","isoabbreviation":"Nutrients","nlmid":"101521595"},"printPublicationDate":"2019-09-01"},"pubYear":"2019","pageInfo":"E2146","abstractText":"The epithelial barrier is the frontline defense against enteropathogenic bacteria and nutrition-linked xenobiotic stressors in the alimentary tract. In particular, enteropathogenic <i>Escherichia coli</i> (EPEC) insults the gut barrier and is increasingly implicated in chronic intestinal diseases such as inflammatory bowel disease. For the efficient development of intervention against barrier-linked distress, the present study provided a <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i>-based assessment instead of extensive preclinical evaluations using mammalian models. In particular, EPEC infected the gut and shortened the lifespan of <i>C. elegans</i>, which was counteracted by colonization of <i>E. coli</i> strain Nissle 1917 (EcN). In addition to the competitive actions of EcN against EPEC, EcN improved the gut barrier integrity of worms via the Zonula occludens ortholog (Zoo-1) induction, which was verified in the murine infection and colitis model. The worm-based assessment provided a crucial methodology and important insights into the potent chronic events in the human gut barrier after the ingestion of probiotic candidates as a mucoactive dietary or therapeutic agent.","affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan 50612, Korea. 1022myths@hanmail.net.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grantId":"2018R1D1A3B05041889","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gastrointestinal Tract","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intestinal Mucosa","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_Y