"hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2020-03-03","dateOfCreation":"2019-09-11","dateOfRevision":"2020-03-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2019-09-08","firstPublicationDate":"2019-09-08","firstIndexDate":"2019-09-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"36470131","source":"MED","pmid":"36470131","pmcid":"PMC9720106","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC9720106"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.redox.2022.102565","title":"Xenobiotic-induced ribosomal stress compromises dysbiotic gut barrier aging: A one health perspective.","authorString":"Sun J, Kim J, Jeong H, Kwon D, Moon Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sun J","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Junjie","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Integrative Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan, 50612, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Kim J","lastName":"Kim","firstName":"Juil","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Integrative Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan, 50612, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Jeong H","lastName":"Jeong","firstName":"Hoyoung","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4994-7556"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Integrative Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan, 50612, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Kwon D","lastName":"Kwon","firstName":"Dasom","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Integrative Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan, 50612, South Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Moon Y","lastName":"Moon","firstName":"Yuseok","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Integrative Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan, 50612, South Korea; Graduate Program of Genomic Data Sciences, Pusan National University, Yangsan, 50612, South Korea. Electronic address: moon@pnu.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4994-7556"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"59","journalIssueId":3489471,"dateOfPublication":"2023 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2023,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Redox Biol","title":"Redox biology","essn":"2213-2317","issn":"2213-2317","isoabbreviation":"Redox Biol","nlmid":"101605639"},"printPublicationDate":"2023-02-01"},"pubYear":"2023","pageInfo":"102565","abstractText":"Upon exposure to internal or environmental insults, ribosomes stand sentinel. In particular, stress-driven dysregulation of ribosomal homeostasis is a potent trigger of adverse outcomes in mammalians. The present study assessed whether the ribosomal insult affects the aging process via the regulation of sentinel organs such as the gut. Analyses of the human aging dataset demonstrated that elevated features of ribosomal stress are inversely linked to barrier maintenance biomarkers during the aging process. Ribosome-insulted worms displayed reduced lifespan, which was associated with the disruption of gut barriers. Mechanistically, ribosomal stress-activated Sek-1/p38 signaling, a central platform of ribosomal stress responses, counteracted the gut barrier deterioration through the maintenance of the gut barrier, which was consistent with the results in a murine insult model. However, since the gut-protective p38 signaling was attenuated with aging, the ribosomal stress-induced distress was exacerbated in the gut epithelia and mucosa of the aged animals, subsequently leading to increased bacterial exposure. Moreover, the bacterial community-based evaluation predicted concomitant increases in the abundance of mucosal sugar utilizers and mucin metabolic enzymes in response to ribosomal insult in the aged host. All of the present evidence on ribosomal insulting against the gut barrier integrity from worms to mammals provides new insights into organelle-associated translational modulation of biological longevity in a one health perspective.","affiliation":"Laboratory of Mucosal Exposome and Biomodulation, Department of Integrative Biomedical Sciences and Biomedical Research Institute, Pusan National University, Yangsan, 50612, South Korea.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Ministry of Education","grantId":"2018R1D1A3B05041889","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Research Foundation of Korea","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ribosomes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mammals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Xenobiotics","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Signal Transduction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aging"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Longevity"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"One Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Deoxynivalenol","Microbiota","Gut Barrier","Ribosomal Stress","Gut Aging"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Xenobiotics","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"doi","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.redox.2022.102565"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"doi","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.redox.2022.102565"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"doi","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.redox.2022.102565"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.redox.2022.102565"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9720106"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9720106?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":13,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2023-01-17","dateOfCreation":"2022-12-05","dateOfRevision":"2023-02-21","electronicPublicationDate":"2022-11-30","firstPublicationDate":"2022-11-30","firstIndexDate":"2022-12-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2022-12-07","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"24677669","source":"MED","pmid":"24677669","pmcid":"PMC4717472","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC4717472"]},"doi":"10.1002/cyto.a.22450","title":"Quantitative absorption cytometry for measuring red blood cell hemoglobin mass and volume.","authorString":"Schonbrun E, Malka R, Di Caprio G, Schaak D, Higgins JM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Schonbrun E","lastName":"Schonbrun","firstName":"Ethan","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Rowland Institute at Harvard, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Malka R","lastName":"Malka","firstName":"Roy","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Di Caprio G","lastName":"Di Caprio","firstName":"Giuseppe","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5564-8064"}},{"fullName":"Schaak D","lastName":"Schaak","firstName":"Diane","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Higgins JM","lastName":"Higgins","firstName":"John M","initials":"JM"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5564-8064"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"85","journalIssueId":2144063,"dateOfPublication":"2014 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2014,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cytometry A","title":"Cytometry. Part A : the journal of the International Society for Analytical Cytology","essn":"1552-4930","issn":"1552-4922","isoabbreviation":"Cytometry A","nlmid":"101235694"},"printPublicationDate":"2014-04-01"},"pubYear":"2014","pageInfo":"332-338","abstractText":"We present an optical system, called the quantitative absorption cytometer (QAC), to measure the volume and hemoglobin mass of red blood cells flowing through a microfluidic channel. In contrast to clinical hematology analyzers, where cells are sphered in order for both volume and hemoglobin to be measured accurately, the QAC measures cells in their normal physiological shape. Human red blood cells are suspended in a refractive index-matching absorbing buffer, driven through a microfluidic channel, and imaged using a transmission light microscope onto a color camera. A red and a blue LED illuminate cells and images at each color are used to independently retrieve cell volume and hemoglobin mass. This system shows good agreement with red blood cell indices retrieved by a clinical hematology analyzer and in fact measures a smaller coefficient of variation of hemoglobin concentration. In addition to cell indices, the QAC returns height and mass maps of each measured cell. These quantitative images are valuable for analyzing the detailed morphology of individual cells as well as statistical outliers found in the data. We also measured red blood cells in hypertonic and hypotonic buffers to quantify the correlation between volume and hemoglobin mass under osmotic stress. Because this method is invariant to cell shape, even extremely nonspherical cells in hypertonic buffers can be measured accurately.","affiliation":"Rowland Institute at Harvard, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"DP2 DK098087","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"U01 HL114476","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Erythrocytes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hemoglobins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"AN","qualifierName":"analysis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Erythrocyte Indices"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Microfluidic Analytical Techniques","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Flow Cytometry","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation